**Proposition 10.1 (A necessary algebraic restriction for the joint
problem).** Every actual base in $D_M$, and therefore every base in
$D_J\cap D_M$, lies in $\mathcal B_M\cap\mathbb R^2$. In particular
the original strict-real joint incidence has a proper algebraic base
restriction. This does not identify the strict-real liftable part of
$\mathcal B_M$.

Proof. For an actual distinct $M$ pair, Proposition 2.4 supplies
the real inverse source-distance guard. Proposition 2.3 supplies
the square slacks and has $Q_M>0$, and $p\ne24$ supplies
the other inverse. Hence it defines a point of $\mathcal Z_M$.
Its base belongs to $\mathcal B_M$, and Theorem 9.2 makes this
a proper algebraic subset of the plane.

A complex square slack proves nonvanishing of its source factor,
not its ordered positivity. The finite-field norms prove generic
complex source reconstruction and nonidentity of rational functions,
not real inequalities. Likewise a point of the saturation variety
may have $Q_M=0$: (8.2) describes a closure, while $hQ_M-1=0$
would impose pointwise nonvanishing. Finally the carrier and base
curve are algebraic necessary conditions. Their real points must
still satisfy the original strict source inequalities, all original
clock equations, the entire-gain degree bound, and both within-pair
distinctness guards before they yield the original joint event.
Thus none of these algebraic facts supplies that missing real lift. ∎

